        String stephan_boltzmann_constant "5.67e-8 W m-2 K-4";
        String earth_radius "6.371e+6 m";
        String angular_speed_of_earth_rotation "7.29245e-5 rad s-1";
        String gravitational_accleration "9.80665 m s-2";
        String gas_constant_for_dry_air "287.04 J K-1 kg-1";
        String specific_heat_of_dry_air_at_constant_pressure "1004.6 J K-1 kg-1";
        String latent_heat_of_vaporization "2.507e+6 J kg-1";
        String solar_constant "1365 W m-2";
        String rda_derived_hybrid_level_a_b "Model half-levels, which bracket the model levels (model half-levels and model levels are denoted as 'hybrid_half_level' and 'hybrid_level', respectively, in the coordinate variables), number k=1 to k=101 arranged from the surface to the nominal top of atmosphere (TOA).The pressure at half-levels (also known as 'interfaces') is given by p_k = a_k + b_k*ps, k=1,2,...,101, where ps is the surface pressure in Pa, a_k has units of Pa, b_k is dimesionless, and a_k and b_k at model half-levels are obtained from https://jra.kishou.go.jp/JRA-3Q/document/JRA-3Q_TL479_format_en.pdf, p. 26-28. (See the variables 'a_hybrid_half_level' and 'b_hybrid_half_level'). The pressure at model levels (also known as full levels), k=1 to k=100, is given by Pml_k = exp{[(p_k*ln(p_k) - p_(k+1)*ln(p_(k+1))]/[p_k - p_(k+1)] - 1}, k=1,2,...,99 (3.18 S+B) and Pml_100 = 0.5*p_100. (Simmons, A. J., and D. M. Burridge, 1981: An energy and angular-momentum conserving finite-difference scheme and hybrid vertical coordinates. Mon. Wea. Rev., 109(4), 758-766, eq 3.18. https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/mwre/109/4/1520-0493_1981_109_0758_aeaamc_2_0_co_2.xml)As a check, one can refer to the variable designated as 'p_hybrid_level_ps_1000mb' which provides a set of pressure values at model levels (hybrid_level) for a surface pressure of 1000mb. Notice that Pml_k is a function of ps, surface pressure. Thus for each model level k=1,2,...,100, one can compute a set of (ps,Pml_k) = (x,y) for a range of surface pressure values, and regress (ps,Pml_k) to find a_k (intercept in Pa) and b_k (slope) for the given model level k. This computation has been performed by Dave Stepaniak <davestep@ucar.edu> using scipy.stats.linregress, version 1.10.0 (see the variables 'a_hybrid_level' and 'b_hybrid_level'). (Supplemental information can also be found in Trenberth, K. E., J. C. Berry, and L. E. Buja, 1993: Vertical interpolation and truncation of model-coordinate data. NCAR Technical Note NCAR/TN-396+STR, 54 pp.)";
        UInt32 _ChunkSizes 1, 34, 160, 320;
